    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-351664",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e367aafb-2474-5dfc-a45f-be3a5ea76942",
      "knowledge_point": "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Two Steps",
      "problem": "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+2,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{3}{2}).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y\\,(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y\\,(\\frac{3}{2}).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is $\\frac{3}{2},$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2}-0)}{2}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+2=2\\cdot 0+2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =1+Δy=1+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+2=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+2=\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{21}{8} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{21}{8}=\\frac{41}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{8}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{41}{8}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y\\,(\\frac{3}{2})≈\\frac{41}{8}.$",
