      "knowledge_point": "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Two Steps",
      "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=1$ and $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}={1}^{2}-{2}^{2}=-3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=(-3)\\cdot 1=-3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2-3=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $-3$ |\n| $2$ | $-1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}={2}^{2}-(-1)^{2}=3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 1=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+3=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $-3$ |\n| $2$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $3$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈2.$",
      "answer_fields": [
