      "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}x}{2},\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(2).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}x}{2}=\\frac{{2}^{2}\\cdot 0}{2}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+0=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}x}{2}=\\frac{{2}^{2}\\cdot 1}{2}=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈4.$",
