    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-351665",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e367aafb-2474-5dfc-a45f-be3a5ea76942",
      "knowledge_point": "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Two Steps",
      "problem": "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x+4,\\,y(1)=0.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(4).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(4)≈$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(4).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=4$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=0.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+4=4\\cdot 1+4=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}=12 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =0+Δy=0+12=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $12$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+4=4\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+4=14 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=14\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}=21 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=12+21=33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $12$ | $14$ | $21$ |\n| $4$ | $33$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y(4)≈33.$",
