      "knowledge_point": "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Three Steps",
      "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(x-1),\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(4).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(4).$ The distance from $x=1$ and $x=4$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-1)=2\\cdot (1-1)=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+0=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-1)=2\\cdot (2-1)=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-1)=4\\cdot (3-1)=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot 1=8 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+1=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=4+8=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table. \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $8$ | $8$ |\n| $4$ | $12$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(4)≈12.$",
