    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-348078",
      "knowledge_point_id": "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87",
      "knowledge_point": "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Three Steps",
      "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+3y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(6).$ \n\n$y(6)≈$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(6).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=6$ is $6,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{6-0}{3}=\\frac{6}{3}=2\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+3y={0}^{2}+3(1)=3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 2=6 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+2=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+6=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+3y={2}^{2}+3(7)=25 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=25\\cdot 2=50 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+2=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=7+50=57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ | $25$ | $50$ |\n| $4$ | $57$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+3y={4}^{2}+3(57)=187 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=187\\cdot 2=374 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=4+2=6 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=57+374=431\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ | $25$ | $50$ |\n| $4$ | $57$ | $187$ | $374$ |\n| $6$ | $431$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(6)≈431.$",
