    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-7701",
      "knowledge_point_id": "8e53619d-67f3-5992-9280-0f884214eca6",
      "knowledge_point": "Completing the Second Row of a Table Using Euler's Method",
      "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny'=xy^4, \\qquad y(2) = 1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $\\Delta y$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\vphantom{1}$ |  |  | $\\, ? \\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$\\Delta{y}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{y}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot (2)^{4} \\\\  & =40 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =40\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.  \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $2$ | $40$ | $20$ |\n\nTherefore, the missing value is $\\Delta y = 20.$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-537",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e367aafb-2474-5dfc-a45f-be3a5ea76942",
      "knowledge_point": "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Two Steps",
      "problem": "Given the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = 2xy, \\qquad y(1)=3,\n$$\n\nuse Euler's method to find an approximation to $y(2)$ using two steps of equal size.",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=3.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy=2\\cdot 1\\cdot 3=6 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+3=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y' \\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ | $6$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 6=18 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=18\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=9 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=6+9=15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ | $6$ | $18$ | $9$ |\n| $2$ | $15$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y(2) \\approx 15.$",
